@@ -69,6 +69,31 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Rich can pretty print a JSON document supplied as text with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json`. The output is the JSON re-serialized over multiple lines with an indent of 4 spaces by default, and it is syntax highlighted unless you pass ``highlight=False``::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The indent can be changed with the ``indent`` argument, and highlighting can be disabled with ``highlight``.
+
+The JSON renderable disables word wrapping, so a long value is never wrapped onto several lines. Printed with default options the console still crops lines wider than its width. Pass ``soft_wrap=True`` to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` if you want the full lines kept.
+
+Logging a JSON renderable works the same way. Import the ``JSON`` class from :mod:`rich.json` and pass it to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.log(JSON('{"name": "Rich"}'))
+
+You can also use the package-level :func:`rich.print_json` function, which prints with the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('[1, 2, 3]')
+
+To pretty print a JSON file from the command line, run the ``rich.json`` module with the path to the file::
+
+    $ python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
